  "chapter": 271,
  "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
  "text": "\n                APPROVAL MEMORANDUM - No. 3  Chapter 271\n\n      MEMORANDUM filed with Assembly Bill Number 2115-B, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the executive law and the general municipal law, in\n        relation  to  requiring certain police officers to be re-trained\n        in cardiopulmonary resuscitation every two years\"\n\n    APPROVED\n\n  Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR) is a life-saving technique  admin-\nistered  when  someone's  breathing  or heartbeat has stopped. CPR keeps\noxygenated blood flowing to the brain and other vital organs, which  can\nkeep  an  individual  alive  until  additional medical assistance can be\nprovided.\n\n  In 2010, Briana Ojeda suffered an asthma attack  while  playing  at  a\nlocal  playground.  In route to the hospital, Brian's mother was stopped\nby a local police officer who was unable to perform  CPR.  Briana  trag-\nically passed away shortly after she arrived at the hospital.\n\n  I  fully  support  the  intent of this legislation, known as \"Briana's\nLaw.\"  This bill will save lives by ensuring that, by law, New York City\nPolice Department and New  York  State  Police  candidates  receive  CPR\ntraining  prior to graduation, and then require troopers and officers to\nbe retrained and tested in CPR every two years. The  Executive  and  the\nLegislature  have  agreed to a chapter amendment to ensure that Briana's\nLaw can be implemented effectively. On that basis,  I  am  signing  this\nbill.\n\n  This bill is approved.                      (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                                ______\n",
  "year": 2017
 },
